Subject: [sshmap fork 0006] a malformed CSI is ignored, not raised (the TypeError class of patch 0004)

Provenance: the published defect report BUG-A/BUG-B of the pyte audit
            (github.com/org-ai-assisted/pyte-audit, `BUGS/BUG-AB-malformed-csi-crash.md`, the
            tree F:\PythonAI\pyte_org-ai-assisted_fork) — the report's own proposed fix, which
            upstream master (0.8.3.dev) does NOT carry: this class is unfixed upstream AND in
            that fork. The report's finding, re-measured here against the vendored fork: the
            CSI branch of `_parser_fsm()` forwards EVERY collected parameter to the mapped
            handler (`csi_dispatch[char](*params)`, and `(*params, private=True)` for every
            `?`-prefixed CSI) while the handlers have fixed signatures, so a sequence with a
            surplus parameter raises `TypeError` out of `feed()` — measured over the fork's own
            CSI table (27 finals): 19 finals for the surplus shape (`ESC[1;2A` ->
            `cursor_up() takes from 1 to 2 positional arguments but 3 were given`) and 19 for
            the private marker (`ESC[?0A` -> `cursor_up() got an unexpected keyword argument
            'private'`). The crash reaches the canvas as the logged "terminal: feed failed" with
            the tail of the chunk lost — the shape the v1.5.7 tester hit with `mc` (patch 0004,
            which closed exactly two handlers of the class: `? r` and `? n`).
            The alternative considered and NOT taken: widening ~21 single-argument handlers with
            `*args, private=False`. It is a larger diff over the same class, it freezes the
            parser's argument shape into every handler, and it still needs a per-handler audit
            for the new finals; the parser-level guard tolerates the class in ONE place. The
            accepted cost, stated in the code comment as well: a `TypeError` raised INSIDE a
            handler is routed to `debug` instead of escaping `feed()` — pyte's handlers are
            small and the debug sink records the sequence, so the loss of a traceback is the
            cheaper half of the trade.
Base:       pyte 0.8.2 (PyPI sdist, sha256 in MANIFEST.md), pyte/streams.py (after patches 0001-0005).
Policy:     drop when upstream makes the private dispatch and the surplus parameters tolerant
            (0.8.3+); the report declares the defect open in master, so until then the fork
            carries it.
Applied:    2026-09-26, sshmap v1.5.7.1.

diff --git a/pyte/streams.py b/pyte/streams.py
index cd0f51c..c94da87 100644
--- a/pyte/streams.py
+++ b/pyte/streams.py
@@ -349,10 +349,24 @@ class Stream:
                         if char == ";":
                             current = ""
                         else:
-                            if private:
-                                csi_dispatch[char](*params, private=True)
-                            else:
-                                csi_dispatch[char](*params)
+                            # sshmap fork (patch 0006): the mapped handlers have FIXED
+                            # signatures, so a surplus numeric parameter (`ESC[1;2A` ->
+                            # ``cursor_up(1, 2)``) or a private marker on a command that has no
+                            # private form (`ESC[?0A` -> ``cursor_up(0, private=True)``) raised
+                            # ``TypeError`` out of ``feed()`` and aborted the whole chunk. A
+                            # terminal ignores what it does not understand: the failed dispatch
+                            # goes to the catch-all ``debug`` sink — the SAME endpoint the
+                            # dispatcher already uses for an unknown final byte. The cost of the
+                            # guard is stated in the patch header: a ``TypeError`` raised INSIDE a
+                            # handler is swallowed too (the handlers are simple enough that this is
+                            # the cheaper half of the trade).
+                            try:
+                                if private:
+                                    csi_dispatch[char](*params, private=True)
+                                else:
+                                    csi_dispatch[char](*params)
+                            except TypeError:
+                                debug(*params, private=private)
                             break  # CSI is finished.
             elif char == OSC_C1:
                 code = yield None
